          "text": "{\"relation\":\"REFINED\",\"consensus_summary\":\"Mercy is chiefly God’s compassionate lovingkindness toward sinners and sufferers, expressed in forgiveness, withheld judgment, and active aid; it also includes covenant fidelity and obligates recipients to practice mercy toward others.\",\"book_summary\":\"Mercy is covenant loyalty manifested through compassion, forgiveness, and faithful dealing. Receiving mercy entails reciprocal mercy, while pardon is associated with confession and forsaking sin.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Divine compassionate lovingkindness that forgives, spares, and helps, with human mercy as its ethical imitation.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Covenantally faithful compassion and forgiveness that carries obligations of repentance and reciprocal mercy.\",\"core_relation\":\"COMPATIBLE_OVERLAP\",\"extension_relation\":\"BOOK_SUBSET\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The book restricts or more tightly frames mercy by emphasizing covenant fidelity, confession and forsaking of sin, and the recipient’s obligation to extend mercy. The consensus includes these themes but also applies mercy more broadly to compassionate aid and undeserved pardon without making those covenantal and ethical conditions part of every case.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"There is no contradiction in the central identification: both understand mercy as compassionate forgiveness and faithful lovingkindness. The material difference is that the book resists treating mercy as merely unconditional non-punishment and makes covenantal response and reciprocal conduct definitional rather than secondary.\",\"rationale\":\"The book preserves the consensus referent—divine compassion, forgiveness, and its human imitation—but materially sharpens its boundaries. Covenant loyalty was already a common consensus alternative, and the obligation to show mercy was also explicitly recognized, so the entry is neither novel nor divergent. Its integration of these elements as necessary framing, together with confession and forsaking, makes it a refinement rather than a mere wording match.\",\"citation_support\":\"STRONG\",\"strongest_objection\":\"The cited texts strongly illustrate covenant mercy, repentant reception of mercy, and reciprocal mercy, but they may not establish that these conditions define every biblical use; other passages portray mercy toward sufferers or sinners without explicitly mentioning covenant obedience, confession, or prior reciprocal conduct.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
      ],
